Fetch (Module 4, lesson 9's pattern, requiring manual steps):

async function getUser(id: number) {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
  return response.json();
}

Axios (npm install axios), with automatic JSON parsing and automatic error-throwing:

import axios from 'axios';

async function getUser(id: number) {
  const response = await axios.get(`/api/users/${id}`);
  return response.data; // already parsed - no .json() call needed,
                          // and a non-2xx status would have already
                          // thrown an error automatically, no manual
                          // response.ok check required
}

Axios interceptors, directly extending Module 10, lesson 2's centralized auth-header and 401-handling logic into a built-in mechanism:

const api = axios.create({ baseURL: '/api' });

api.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  const token = getStoredToken();
  if (token) config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  return config;
});

api.interceptors.response.use(
  (response) => response,
  (error) => {
    if (error.response?.status === 401) clearStoredToken();
    return Promise.reject(error);
  }
);

