A GraphQL query, specifying exactly the fields needed, directly solving over-fetching:

query {
  user(id: "42") {
    name
    email
    posts {
      title
    }
  }
}

This single query also directly solves under-fetching: a user AND their posts' titles, in one request, where REST would typically require GET /users/42 followed by a separate GET /users/42/posts.

Sending this query from JavaScript (using fetch, since GraphQL ultimately still travels over regular HTTP POST requests):

async function getUser(id: string) {
  const response = await fetch('/graphql', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      query: `query { user(id: "${id}") { name email posts { title } } }`
    })
  });
  const result = await response.json();
  return result.data.user;
}

A mutation, GraphQL's equivalent of a REST POST/PATCH/DELETE:

mutation {
  createPost(title: "New Post", content: "...") {
    id
    title
  }
}

